00:10:08,720 --> 00:10:11,560
Day one of kindergarten, there's a bunch of things we had to do

262
00:10:11,560 --> 00:10:14,200
where if you didn't do them, there was a big problem.

263
00:10:14,200 --> 00:10:16,720
And, you might even be held back a grade.

264
00:10:16,720 --> 00:10:18,600
And yet, when you need, so, like, what's that?

265
00:10:18,600 --> 00:10:20,800
Like, singing and dancing?

266
00:10:20,800 --> 00:10:26,160
Yeah. So, when my kids were in grades four through six,

267
00:10:26,160 --> 00:10:30,560
they had to do three different music classes.

268
00:10:30,560 --> 00:10:34,080
They had to do chorus, then music appreciation,

269
00:10:34,080 --> 00:10:37,000
and dance, all three.

270
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:38,760
I actually tried calling up the school and saying,

271
00:10:38,760 --> 00:10:40,760
is there any way to get out of any of this?

272
00:10:40,760 --> 00:10:43,160
Could they just go to the library?

273
00:10:43,160 --> 00:10:45,360
And, no, there's no way to get out of it.

274
00:10:45,360 --> 00:10:49,240
You must do this stuff because there's so much

275
00:10:49,240 --> 00:10:51,760
of the modern economy is based on music and dancing.

276
00:10:51,760 --> 00:10:53,560
Now, you know, a lot of people want to say, oh, but it's,

277
00:10:53,560 --> 00:10:54,920
you know, like, people learn

278
00:10:54,920 --> 00:10:57,160
and people enjoy these subjects.

279
00:10:57,160 --> 00:10:59,240
First of all, there's a lot of people who don't enjoy them

280
00:10:59,240 --> 00:11:01,560
at all who are totally miserable, right?

281
00:11:01,560 --> 00:11:03,760
If you go and look at any mandatory dance class

282
00:11:03,760 --> 00:11:06,400
for young kids and look it out, the expressions on the boys' faces,

283
00:11:06,400 --> 00:11:09,840
at least, they are not only unhappy, they are having some

284
00:11:09,840 --> 00:11:12,960
of the worst experiences they will ever have in their lives.

285
00:11:12,960 --> 00:11:17,200
I've seen boys being forced to dance just like this.

286
00:11:17,200 --> 00:11:19,800
Like, not just one or two, they're just like,

287
00:11:19,800 --> 00:11:22,960
they're just so miserable and so humiliated.

288
00:11:22,960 --> 00:11:24,240
And yet, they have to do it.

289
00:11:24,240 --> 00:11:28,840
So, in any case, no matter how much most people enjoy it,

290
00:11:28,840 --> 00:11:31,840
there's still the puzzle, why do employers care?

291
00:11:31,840 --> 00:11:33,600
Do employers, are employers supposed to care

292
00:11:33,600 --> 00:11:36,880
if you enjoyed yourself when you were in kindergarten?

293
00:11:36,880 --> 00:11:39,880
No, they're supposed to care about your marginal productivity.

294
00:11:39,880 --> 00:11:43,000
So, unless you're going to say that these dance classes are

295
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:47,320
somehow raising productivity in non-dance areas,

296
00:11:47,320 --> 00:11:50,880
seems like a real stretch, right?

297
00:11:50,880 --> 00:11:53,320
Again, there's always people to say, well, look, you can't,

298
00:11:53,320 --> 00:11:55,080
well, like, you know, like, who knows what

299
00:11:55,080 --> 00:11:57,200
if that raises productivity?

300
00:11:57,200 --> 00:11:59,360
Well, I suppose we don't have any definitive proof

301
00:11:59,360 --> 00:12:02,000
that dance doesn't make you a better investment banker.

302
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:04,320
But, it's, that seems like one

303
00:12:04,320 --> 00:12:07,400
of those extraordinary claims requiring extraordinary evidence,

304
00:12:07,400 --> 00:12:08,800
not just something that you would believe

305
00:12:08,800 --> 00:12:09,960
if someone says so.

306
00:12:09,960 --> 00:12:10,520
All right.

307
00:12:10,520 --> 00:12:13,960
So, anyway, first puzzle where I have the longest book I think I'll

308
00:12:13,960 --> 00:12:17,160
ever write, why does non-job related schooling still raise

309
00:12:17,160 --> 00:12:18,640
your income?

310
00:12:18,640 --> 00:12:23,920
Or, in the words of the class smart aleck, what does this have

311
00:12:23,920 --> 00:12:24,720
to do with real life?

312
00:12:24,720 --> 00:12:27,040
What does trigonometry have to do with real life?

313
00:12:27,040 --> 00:12:27,160
All right.

314
00:12:27,160 --> 00:12:28,760
Well, now you know, right?

315
00:12:28,760 --> 00:12:31,560
Life has just been one giant trigonometry exam ever since,

316
00:12:31,560 --> 00:12:33,080
right?

317
00:12:33,080 --> 00:12:34,360
Not my experience.

318
00:12:34,360 --> 00:12:35,320
All right.

319
00:12:35,320 --> 00:12:36,480
Now, here are some other ones

320
00:12:36,480 --> 00:12:37,640
where the answer may be more obvious,

321
00:12:37,640 --> 00:12:39,520
but it's all part of the same model.

322
00:12:39,520 --> 00:12:42,040
So, why is it that people often won't buy a good

323
00:12:42,040 --> 00:12:43,320
without a warranty?

324
00:12:43,320 --> 00:12:46,400
Even if you know that you're not going to go and cash

325
00:12:46,400 --> 00:12:48,080
in the warranty or use the warranty.

326
00:12:48,080 --> 00:12:50,440
Like, suppose you're a tourist, you're driving through a town,

327
00:12:50,440 --> 00:12:54,080
you buy a good, and you look to see whether there's a warranty,

328
00:12:54,080 --> 00:12:55,680
even though you know that you're leaving town

329
00:12:55,680 --> 00:12:57,160
and it will be too inconvenient for you

330
00:12:57,160 --> 00:12:58,640
to go and return the product.

331
00:12:58,640 --> 00:13:00,560
Why do you look for a warranty even if you know

332
00:13:00,560 --> 00:13:02,920
that you're not going to be collecting the warranty?

333
00:13:02,920 --> 00:13:04,400
Or, here's one.

334
00:13:04,400 --> 00:13:07,480
Why use nice paper on a job application?

335
00:13:07,480 --> 00:13:10,280
So, when you are sending out resumes, people often advise you

336
00:13:10,280 --> 00:13:14,480
to go and get the nice paper, the 25% cotton paper.

337
00:13:14,480 --> 00:13:15,360
What's the big deal?

338
00:13:15,360 --> 00:13:17,720
Do you really think there's a person opens up the envelope

339
00:13:17,720 --> 00:13:19,480
and goes, oh, I like the paper.

340
00:13:19,480 --> 00:13:20,600
It's so good.

341
00:13:20,600 --> 00:13:22,080
I love this person.

342
00:13:22,080 --> 00:13:23,120
Is that the story?

343
00:13:23,120 --> 00:13:24,120
It seems hard to believe.

344
00:13:24,120 --> 00:13:26,360
That's the story, right?

345
00:13:26,360 --> 00:13:28,200
Or, why is it there's so many jobs where you have

346
00:13:28,200 --> 00:13:30,880
to wear a suit to work, right?

347
00:13:30,880 --> 00:13:32,200
Somewhere you deal with the public,

348
00:13:32,200 --> 00:13:34,080
you might think the public cares about how you look,

349
00:13:34,080 --> 00:13:35,840
but what about ones where you don't?

350
00:13:35,840 --> 00:13:37,840
Why do you have to wear the suit, which has got to be one

351
00:13:37,840 --> 00:13:40,200
of the dumbest uniforms ever made?

352
00:13:40,200 --> 00:13:43,360
Especially like summer in D.C., a lot of people were wearing suits

353
00:13:43,360 --> 00:13:45,760
in D.C. in the summer.

354
00:13:45,760 --> 00:13:48,280
Why not just say everyone has to wear an Eskimo outfit

355
00:13:48,280 --> 00:13:49,640
or something like that?

356
00:13:49,640 --> 00:13:52,080
Why is that the uniform?

357
00:13:52,080 --> 00:13:55,880
It's so uncomfortable, it's so unpleasant, it's horrible.

358
00:13:55,880 --> 00:13:58,480
But a lot of jobs, if you just say, look,

359
00:13:58,480 --> 00:14:01,400
I just don't wear that uniform, I don't like it, well,

360
00:14:01,400 --> 00:14:03,600
then you're going to not like it in a different place

361
00:14:03,600 --> 00:14:06,440
of employment because we're not hiring you.

362
00:14:06,440 --> 00:14:07,560
All right.

363
00:14:07,560 --> 00:14:08,360
Here's another one.

364
00:14:08,360 --> 00:14:10,320
Why are wedding rings so expensive?

365
00:14:10,320 --> 00:14:13,440
How else could two months' salary last forever?

366
00:14:13,440 --> 00:14:14,160
All right.

367
00:14:14,160 --> 00:14:16,960
And there's a place like, so that means that when the price

368
00:14:16,960 --> 00:14:19,720
of the rings goes down, I just have to buy a bigger ring.

369
00:14:19,720 --> 00:14:24,160
It's kind of a weird framing, and yet, it is actually a common attitude.

370
00:14:24,160 --> 00:14:26,720
Right? And in particular, just imagine that someone figures

371
00:14:26,720 --> 00:14:29,440
out a way to make diamonds that are chemically indistinguishable

372
00:14:29,440 --> 00:14:33,440
from natural diamonds, but they can make them for a penny a diamond.

373
00:14:33,440 --> 00:14:37,400
How long will people continue giving diamond engagement rings

374
00:14:37,400 --> 00:14:39,640
after this innovation?

375
00:14:39,640 --> 00:14:42,480
Like a day?

376
00:14:42,480 --> 00:14:44,160
Hours? Right?

377
00:14:44,160 --> 00:14:45,880
So probably not very long.

378
00:14:45,880 --> 00:14:48,920
There's something about it where it's got to be expensive.

379
00:14:48,920 --> 00:14:51,880
Or here's one that was often asked during the 1980s.

380
00:14:51,880 --> 00:14:56,920
Why are there so many countries that have arsenals of weapons sufficient

381
00:14:56,920 --> 00:14:59,200
to destroy their enemies 100 times over?

382
00:14:59,200 --> 00:15:01,480
What's the point of that?

383
00:15:01,480 --> 00:15:04,080
Right? Why would you have weapons that are more

384
00:15:04,080 --> 00:15:06,800
than enough to destroy the world?

385
00:15:06,800 --> 00:15:09,520
Right? And why go and have a big parade where you show them off?

386
00:15:09,520 --> 00:15:12,360
Like there's the classic military parade in Red Square

387
00:15:12,360 --> 00:15:16,080
where they just have like an endless row of scary-looking missiles

388
00:15:16,080 --> 00:15:17,280
and tanks and everything.

389
00:15:17,280 --> 00:15:19,720
And like you don't want to really launch those things.

390
00:15:19,720 --> 00:15:21,320
You'll be dead too.

391
00:15:21,320 --> 00:15:23,920
It's like look at our arsenal.

392
00:15:23,920 --> 00:15:25,360
Look. Right?

393
00:15:25,360 --> 00:15:27,880
And then here's the last one, which may seem really weird,

394
00:15:27,880 --> 00:15:31,000
but it's also been resolved using the same model.

395
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,600
Why do male peacocks have such large tails?

396
00:15:33,600 --> 00:15:36,440
So have you ever been in a place that had both male peacocks

397
00:15:36,440 --> 00:15:39,440
and then female peahens, as they are called?

398
00:15:39,440 --> 00:15:41,840
So what do peahens look like?

399
00:15:41,840 --> 00:15:43,520
Yeah. Yeah.

400
00:15:43,520 --> 00:15:45,160
Yeah, they're like ugly chickens.

401
00:15:45,160 --> 00:15:45,440
All right?

402
00:15:45,440 --> 00:15:46,000
Like small.

403
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:48,120
Like they don't look like, like you would never recognize

404
00:15:48,120 --> 00:15:48,920
as peacocks.

405
00:15:48,920 --> 00:15:50,640
They're just like, what is this thing?

406
00:15:50,640 --> 00:15:53,560
And then there's the peacocks going around with the tails,

407
00:15:53,560 --> 00:15:55,920
some of them like 15 feet long.

408
00:15:55,920 --> 00:15:59,640
And we're supposed to believe natural selection said

409
00:15:59,640 --> 00:16:02,080
that that's what's going to happen.

410
00:16:02,080 --> 00:16:03,480
I mean the peahens, like yeah, sure,

411
00:16:03,480 --> 00:16:05,360
they're like designed to survive.

412
00:16:05,360 --> 00:16:08,160
Right? But the peacocks look like they're designed to die.

413
00:16:08,160 --> 00:16:10,640
Like look at these things.

414
00:16:10,640 --> 00:16:13,480
Like anyone could catch these animals.

415
00:16:13,480 --> 00:16:15,960
Right? And like what are they, killing wolves?

416
00:16:15,960 --> 00:16:16,800
With their feathers?

417
00:16:16,800 --> 00:16:18,880
Like what in the world is going on?

418
00:16:18,880 --> 00:16:20,480
What is this creature?

419
00:16:20,480 --> 00:16:22,680
Which, is Darwin wrong?

420
00:16:22,680 --> 00:16:24,280
Like this creature could be here?

421
00:16:24,280 --> 00:16:26,360
Seems like it's a great counter example.

422
00:16:26,360 --> 00:16:29,600
It's an animal that is selected for almost instant death

423
00:16:29,600 --> 00:16:30,840
in the wild.

424
00:16:30,840 --> 00:16:32,360
Yet there they are.

425
00:16:32,360 --> 00:16:34,040
So how can that be?

426
00:16:34,040 --> 00:16:34,440
All right.

427
00:16:34,440 --> 00:16:36,760
So now we have these puzzles on the table.

428
00:16:36,760 --> 00:16:38,360
Why don't we take our 10 minute break,

429
00:16:38,360 --> 00:16:40,360
during which time you may resolve all the puzzles

430
00:16:40,360 --> 00:16:42,600
or anticipate the resolution.

431
00:16:42,600 --> 00:16:43,200
Ah, yes, yes.

432
00:16:43,200 --> 00:16:45,120
And why don't we go and turn in homework.

433
00:16:45,120 --> 00:16:48,640
And you can also at this point pick up the answer key

434
00:16:48,640 --> 00:16:52,200
for homework six, as well as homework seven.

435
00:16:52,200 --> 00:17:19,480
All right, let's get back to work, please.

436
00:17:19,480 --> 00:17:21,520
All right, let's get back to work.

437
00:17:21,520 --> 00:17:23,840
All right, so before the break we had the cliffhanger.

438
00:17:23,840 --> 00:17:25,160
And you've all been asking yourself,

439
00:17:25,160 --> 00:17:27,640
how will the economist solve these incredibly difficult

440
00:17:27,640 --> 00:17:28,720
seven puzzles?

441
00:17:28,720 --> 00:17:31,640
How will they, will he be, will he throw up his arms

442
00:17:31,640 --> 00:17:32,880
in total confusion?

443
00:17:32,880 --> 00:17:35,840
Or will he have devised a theory that explains each

444
00:17:35,840 --> 00:17:36,960
and every case?

445
00:17:36,960 --> 00:17:39,080
Right? And of course, the answer is, we've got a theory

446
00:17:39,080 --> 00:17:40,680
to explain each and every case.

447
00:17:40,680 --> 00:17:43,280
So at least a popular way to resolve all

448
00:17:43,280 --> 00:17:45,560
of these seemingly paradoxical results,

449
00:17:45,560 --> 00:17:48,280
those are the name of signaling.

450
00:17:48,280 --> 00:17:50,160
There's lots of signaling models,

451
00:17:50,160 --> 00:17:53,200
but they've all got three basic assumptions.

452
00:17:53,200 --> 00:17:54,520
First of all, there's the assumption

453
00:17:54,520 --> 00:17:57,640
that there are different types of people, firms, animals,

454
00:17:57,640 --> 00:17:58,800
things.

455
00:17:58,800 --> 00:18:01,680
All right, so the types might be able versus unable,

456
00:18:01,680 --> 00:18:03,840
smart versus dumb, honest and dishonest,

457
00:18:03,840 --> 00:18:06,960
hardworking and lazy, all sorts of possible types

458
00:18:06,960 --> 00:18:08,240
out there in the world.

459
00:18:08,240 --> 00:18:10,200
All right, so that's step one.

460
00:18:10,200 --> 00:18:12,920
All right, step two is that we've

461
00:18:12,920 --> 00:18:14,520
got asymmetric information about type.

462
00:18:14,520 --> 00:18:15,020
Why?

463
00:18:20,080 --> 00:18:24,040
Because you know whether you're hardworking or lazy, probably.

464
00:18:24,040 --> 00:18:26,600
But if you go to interview for a job,

465
00:18:26,600 --> 00:18:29,640
and they ask you, are you A, hardworking, or B, lazy?

466
00:18:29,640 --> 00:18:32,120
What does almost everybody say?

467
00:18:32,120 --> 00:18:32,720
Hardworking.

468
00:18:32,720 --> 00:18:34,880
Everyone's hardworking, which means

469
00:18:34,880 --> 00:18:36,400
that there's asymmetric information,

470
00:18:36,400 --> 00:18:38,600
because you know whether you're hardworking or not.

471
00:18:38,600 --> 00:18:41,360
But as far as employers are concerned, on the surface,

472
00:18:41,360 --> 00:18:43,280
everybody looks the same.

473
00:18:43,280 --> 00:18:45,280
It's a bunch of people all claiming

474
00:18:45,280 --> 00:18:48,560
to be married to their jobs.

475
00:18:48,560 --> 00:18:50,320
So there's that.

476
00:18:50,320 --> 00:18:52,920
Also hard to observe the types correctly.

477
00:18:52,920 --> 00:18:55,880
And then the last assumption, the key one,

478
00:18:55,880 --> 00:18:58,840
is that different types have different costs

479
00:18:58,840 --> 00:19:00,560
of doing something observable.

480
00:19:00,560 --> 00:19:14,320
So there's got to be different costs in observable activity.

481
00:19:14,320 --> 00:19:16,560
And then when you put these three together,

482
00:19:16,560 --> 00:19:19,400
the result is if you are the good type,

483
00:19:19,400 --> 00:19:20,960
then you know that you are not going

484
00:19:20,960 --> 00:19:24,000
to get credit for being good just because you say I'm good.

485
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:25,700
Instead, what you're going to want to do

486
00:19:25,700 --> 00:19:29,280
is to find something to do that convinces outside observers

487
00:19:29,280 --> 00:19:30,680
that you're good.

488
00:19:30,680 --> 00:19:33,880
You want to do something that would be really

489
00:19:33,880 --> 00:19:36,120
expensive for a bad person to do,

490
00:19:36,120 --> 00:19:38,320
less expensive for a good person to do,

491
00:19:38,320 --> 00:19:42,600
so that it is believable that you actually are good.

492
00:19:42,600 --> 00:19:44,400
That's the whole idea.

493
00:19:44,400 --> 00:19:48,000
Now, if we have something like this,

494
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:51,360
then it can be in the interest of whoever

495
00:19:51,360 --> 00:19:53,440
is in higher demand, whoever gets better treatment

496
00:19:53,440 --> 00:19:58,640
in the world, to go to school and study Aristotle.

497
00:19:58,640 --> 00:20:01,440
Not because you need to know it.


